 ("h2", "11.2 Water"),
 ("ul", ["Uses: drinking, cooking, bathing, washing, growing crops, industry.", "Water covers about **two-thirds of Earth's surface**; most is in **oceans and seas and is saline (salty)** — unfit for domestic, agricultural or industrial use.", "**Freshwater** exists in ice sheets and snow, rivers and lakes, and underground. Ice, snow and groundwater are hard to access; only a **very small fraction** in ponds, rivers, lakes and wells is easily accessible.", "Everyone does not have equal access; in some places people walk long distances for drinking water."]),
 ("h3", "Conserving water and preventing pollution"),
 ("ul", ["Switch off taps when not in use, fix leaks, water plants carefully, **recycle water** and **harvest rainwater**.",
  "**Pollution:** trash, plastic bags and wrappers, and waste from homes and industries dumped into rivers and lakes make water unfit for use.",
  "**Rainwater harvesting** — collecting and storing rainwater for later use. An age-old Indian practice: **stepwells** — **Bawadi in Rajasthan, Vav in Gujarat** (e.g. Toorji ka Jhalra, Jodhpur) store rainwater and water seeping from lakes, ponds and rivers through stone-lined trenches.",
  "**World Water Day** is observed on **22 March**."]),
 ("h2", "11.3 Energy from the Sun"),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "The **Sun is the main source of energy on Earth** — it provides heat and light. Plants use sunlight to make food; animals eat plants (and other animals), so all living beings depend on the Sun. The cow grazing grass gets energy from the Sun *through the grass*, not from standing in sunshine."),
 ("ul", ["Heat uses: drying clothes, chillies, raw mangoes; light for photosynthesis and vision.", "**Solar panels** capture the Sun's energy and produce electricity (rooftops, street lights, traffic signals). **Solar cooker** cooks food; **solar water heater** heats water.", "Without sunlight for days we would need artificial lighting and heating; solar panels would not work."]),
 ("h2", "11.4 Forests"),
 ("ul", ["Forests are large areas with **dense growth of herbs, shrubs and trees**; a natural home of wild animals, birds and insects, giving food and shelter.", "Forest products: wood, fruits, honey, medicinal herbs, gum, bamboo, lac.", "Forest cover is shrinking mainly because of **large-scale tree cutting**; regrowing a forest takes many years, so use forests responsibly.", "Village tradition: do not pluck fruits from trees; collect only fallen ones (nellikai) so animals and birds can eat.", "Roots **hold the soil** and prevent it from being washed away; fallen leaves decay and **enrich the soil** — an example of **recycling in nature**.", "**Van Mahotsav**: week-long forest festival in July when new trees are planted to increase green cover.", "**Chipko movement** (early 1970s, Uttarakhand): local women hugged trees to prevent them from being felled.", "Sacred groves are another Indian tradition of protecting forests."]),
 ("h2", "11.5 Soil, Rocks and Minerals"),
 ("h3", "Soil"),
 ("ul", ["Soil contains sand, pebbles, roots, insects, worms, tiny organisms and decayed plants and animals; colour and texture differ from place to place (Activity 11.4 — wash hands after handling soil).", "**Earthworms** turn and loosen the soil; spaces between particles give air and room for roots.", "Soil is formed by **disintegration of rocks** by the Sun, water and living organisms over **thousands of years**.", "Different soils suit growing plants or making bricks. Soil supports **biodiversity**."]),
 ("h3", "Rocks and minerals"),
 ("ul", ["Rocks are used to build houses, temples, roads, dams, table tops; slate for roofing, laterite as bricks; granite, sandstone and marble are important rocks. Early humans made **hand axes and arrowheads** from stone.", "Rocks are made of **minerals**; metals like aluminium, gold, copper and iron are extracted from minerals. Minerals are used in aircraft, cars, jewellery, cosmetics, electronics. A basic mobile phone contains about a dozen minerals (gold, silver, copper, cobalt etc.).", "Rocks take thousands to millions of years to form — conserve them."]),
